@[edit-confs step]
(Edit with @[$EDITOR FILE] (nano); other editors: @[help editors])
Parts marked [simpleinit] are read only by simpleinit-msb and its init.d
scripts (the default); if you switched to systemd or OpenRC in the
init-system step, follow the [systemd] or [openrc] lines instead (editing the
others does no harm, but they are ignored).

Partitions and mounts: @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/fstab]
Add a line per filesystem below "Add your file systems here" (@[lsblk -f]
shows your partitions); e.g. for a UEFI disk with an ext4 root:
  /dev/nvme0n1p2  /          ext4  defaults  0 1
  /dev/nvme0n1p1  /boot/efi  vfat  defaults  0 2
Fields: device, mount point, filesystem, options, dump (0), fsck order (1 for
the root filesystem, 2 for others, 0 for btrfs and swap). The file itself has
commented examples for ext4, XFS, btrfs subvolumes, the EFI partition, swap
and /tmp in RAM; more in @[guide disks] (also zram).

[simpleinit] Take a look at the files in @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/sysconfig/]
and edit any you feel should be different. Especially, make sure the
@[facilities] file looks correct, and if you selected a custom keymap at the
start of this install, edit the file called @[keymap] in that directory (type
@[cat $ENCHANT_TMP/keymap] to see which you chose).
[systemd] The init-system step copied the keymap and locale from sysconfig to
@[/etc/vconsole.conf] (KEYMAP=) and @[/etc/locale.conf] (LANG=), under
$ENCHANT_TARGET; edit those, or use @[localectl] after first boot.
[openrc] The init-system step copied the keymap to @[/etc/conf.d/keymaps]
(keymap=) and a console font to /etc/conf.d/consolefont, under
$ENCHANT_TARGET.

Hostname: (default: sourcemage) set it with
  @[enchantment hostname NAME]
which writes /etc/hostname and the matching line in /etc/hosts, so the name
resolves without a network (sudo complains when it doesn't).

Networking: [simpleinit, openrc] @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/network/interfaces]
Read at boot by ifup (@[man interfaces]); the file has commented examples for
wired DHCP, Wi-Fi and a fixed address. Interface names: @[ip link] (they are
the same on the installed system). E.g. Wi-Fi, after copying the WPA config:
  @[cp /etc/wpa_supplicant.conf $ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/]
  auto wlan0
  iface wlan0 inet dhcp
          pre-up wpa_supplicant -B -i wlan0 -c /etc/wpa_supplicant.conf
[systemd] systemd-networkd instead (.network files; wpa_supplicant needs a
unit you write for Wi-Fi): see @[todo systemd]. [openrc] Without that
file, DHCP on every interface: see @[todo openrc].

Timezone: (replace "SOME/TIMEZONE" with yours, e.g. America/New_York;
@[ls /usr/share/zoneinfo] lists them)
  @[enchantment chroot ln -sf /usr/share/zoneinfo/SOME/TIMEZONE /etc/localtime]
The hardware clock is assumed to be UTC; if the machine also runs Windows:
[simpleinit] set @[UTC=no] in sysconfig/hwclock; [openrc] set
@[clock="local"] in /etc/conf.d/hwclock; [systemd] run
@[timedatectl set-local-rtc 1] after first boot (it writes /etc/adjtime).

Services: [simpleinit] SMGL starts the executable scripts in
$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/init.d/runlevels/ at boot. The system logger (metalog) is
on; the SSH server, if you installed openssh, is installed but off. To start
it at every boot:
  @[chmod +x $ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/init.d/runlevels/%3/sshd]
[systemd] @[enchantment chroot systemctl enable sshd] (enabling works in the
chroot; logs go to the journal).
[openrc] @[enchantment chroot rc-update add sshd default] (it makes its host
keys at first start; logs go to metalog, as with simpleinit).

User namespaces (sandboxes for programs, needed by web browsers, Flatpak,
rootless containers and systemd) are on, as in most distributions. They have
also been a way into the kernel for local attackers; with simpleinit or
OpenRC you can turn them off by uncommenting
@[user.max_user_namespaces = 0] in @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/sysctl.conf] (the
file explains what that breaks).

Compiling: sorcery runs one compile job at a time unless told otherwise. To
use all of this machine's CPU threads (@[nproc] counts them):
  @[echo "MAKE_NJOBS=$(nproc)" >> $ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/sorcery/local/compile_config]
All threads make big casts fastest, but the machine gets sluggish meanwhile
(browsing, video); if you'll use it while it compiles, leave 2 or 3 free,
e.g. @[$(($(nproc) - 2))] instead of @[$(nproc)]. Later: @[sorcery] -> Option
Menu -> Parallel/Cached/Distributed Compiling -> EXTRA_JOBS.
Compiling again: ccache keeps each compiled file and hands it back when the
same file is compiled again, so a stopped or failed cast, or a recast, skips
the work already done. It helps from the second build on and costs disk
space (up to 5 GB, in /var/cache/compiler). Not in the base system: after
first boot, @[cast ccache], then set @[CCACHE=on] in
/etc/sorcery/local/compile_config (or "Cached" in the menu above).

Type @[next] to go to the next step.
